31. What CAP will eventually ask
Don't memorize this yet. Just understand the setup.
Suppose:
        A
       / \
      /   \
     B     C
Then:
       partition
          X
     B  XXXXX  C
B and C cannot communicate.
Now a client asks B:
WRITE x = 10
What should B do?
Option 1
Accept the write:
B -> x = 10
even though C cannot be updated.
You're prioritizing continued operation.
Option 2
Reject/wait for the write:
B -> "cannot safely accept"
You're prioritizing a stronger consistency guarantee.
That trade-off is where CAP enters.
But notice how much theory we needed before saying "CAP".
________________________________________
32. What you should NOT conclude from Lesson 1
There are several popular statements that are misleading.
❌ "Distributed systems are just multiple servers."
Not enough.
The important part is independent failure + message-based coordination + lack of global state.
❌ "Network partition means the server is down."
No.
Partition:
A alive
B alive
communication broken
❌ "Timeout means server failure."
No.
Timeout only means:
"I did not receive the expected response within my waiting period."
❌ "If data is replicated, all replicas must always be identical."
Not necessarily.
Different consistency models permit different temporary or permanent visibility behaviors.
❌ "More replicas automatically means more consistency."
No.
Replication can improve availability and fault tolerance while making consistency coordination more complicated.
❌ "CAP is about choosing two of three whenever you design a system."
This is one of the biggest CAP misconceptions.
We'll dedicate Lesson 4 to it.
________________________________________
33. The university-level abstraction
If you want one compact model to remember from today's lesson, use this:
Distributed System
│
├── Processes
│     └── independent local state
│
├── Communication
│     └── messages over a network
│
├── Time
│     ├── synchronous
│     ├── asynchronous
│     └── partially synchronous
│
├── Failures
│     ├── crash
│     ├── omission
│     ├── partition
│     └── Byzantine
│
└── Knowledge
      └── each node sees only
          local state + received messages
And the most important principle:
A distributed system cannot directly observe the global state. It must infer what is happening from messages, timeouts, and local observations.
That single idea explains a surprising amount of distributed-systems theory.
