CAP — Lesson 4: Real-World Systems and CAP Misconceptions
Now we put everything together.
The goal of this lesson is to move from:
CAP theory
    ↓
partition
    ↓
C vs A
to:
"Okay, I'm designing a real distributed system.
What does CAP actually tell me?"
This is where many otherwise experienced developers get CAP wrong.
________________________________________
1. First, destroy the biggest misconception
You will often hear:
"CAP says choose any two of Consistency, Availability, and Partition Tolerance."
That's a useful teaching slogan, but it's a poor engineering model.
The more accurate statement is:
When a partition occurs, a distributed system cannot simultaneously guarantee linearizability and availability.
Think:
Normal operation

        C + A
         |
         | network works
         v
     coordination


Partition occurs

A -------- X -------- B

         ↓

   C  OR  A
The key word is:
when a partition occurs
Not:
"You must permanently choose C or A."
________________________________________
2. Misconception #1 — "CA systems are possible"
You'll sometimes see:
CA database
used as a category.
Be careful.
Suppose you have:
Node A <----> Node B
and assume the network never partitions.
You can have:
Consistency ✓
Availability ✓
But the moment:
A ----X---- B
occurs, you have to decide what happens.
If you refuse to handle partitions, then you're effectively saying:
"P is outside my failure model."
That's not a very useful assumption for a distributed system operating over a network.
Therefore:
"CA database" is usually misleading terminology in practical distributed-system discussions.
________________________________________
3. Misconception #2 — "Partition tolerance means the system stays available"
No.
This is extremely important.
Partition tolerance means roughly:
The system has a defined behavior despite communication failures between nodes.
It does not mean:
partition
   ↓
everything continues normally
A CP-style system may respond to a partition by:
partition
   ↓
cannot establish quorum
   ↓
reject writes
That is still partition-tolerant.
The system has chosen a behavior under partition.
So:
Partition tolerance
        ≠
Always available during partition
________________________________________
4. Misconception #3 — "CP means unavailable"
Also wrong.
Imagine:
A <----> B <----> C
Everything works.
A CP-oriented system may be:
100% operational
for normal workloads.
Then:
A ---- B       X       C
A/B have a quorum.
C doesn't.
The C side may stop accepting certain operations.
So:
Normal:

C ✓
A ✓


During partition:

C side -> unavailable
A/B side -> available
Therefore:
CP means consistency is preferred over availability when partition prevents the required coordination.
It does not mean the system is normally unavailable.
________________________________________
5. Misconception #4 — "AP means weak/bad consistency"
No.
An AP-style design may intentionally allow:
A: x=20
B: x=30
temporarily.
But it may have a sophisticated mechanism to eventually reconcile:
A: 30
B: 30
Or merge:
A: [book]
B: [phone]

↓

[book, phone]
This is a consistency contract, not simply "the database is wrong."
Different applications need different guarantees.
________________________________________
6. Misconception #5 — "Eventual consistency means data will eventually become correct"
Not necessarily.
This is a subtle but important distinction.
Eventual consistency generally means:
If updates stop and the system remains healthy, replicas eventually converge.
It doesn't automatically tell you:
•	which value wins 
•	whether conflicting writes are lost 
•	whether user intent is preserved 
•	whether causality is preserved 
•	whether the result is semantically correct 
For example:
Replica A:
balance = ₹900

Replica B:
balance = ₹800
"Last write wins" may converge to:
₹800
But that doesn't mean ₹800 is semantically correct.
Consistency mechanisms and application correctness are different layers.
________________________________________
7. Misconception #6 — "Replication means consistency"
Absolutely not.
Consider:
Primary
   |
   | asynchronous replication
   v
Replica
Primary:
x = 20
Replica:
x = 10
Both are replicas.
They simply aren't synchronized yet.
Replication gives you:
multiple copies
It doesn't automatically give you:
strong consistency
You need a protocol governing:
•	ordering 
•	acknowledgment 
•	commit 
•	failover 
•	leader election 
•	quorum 
•	conflict handling 
________________________________________
8. Misconception #7 — "Synchronous replication solves CAP"
No.
Synchronous replication means something like:
Primary
   |
   | wait for replica
   v
Replica
before acknowledging a write.
This can provide stronger durability/consistency properties.
But what happens if the replica becomes unreachable?
Primary ----X---- Replica
The primary has two choices:
1. Wait/reject
2. Commit anyway
If it waits:
stronger guarantee
        +
less availability
If it commits anyway:
more availability
        +
weaker guarantee
You've encountered CAP again.
Synchronous replication does not repeal the theorem.
________________________________________
9. Real system #1 — Cassandra
Let's start with a system explicitly designed around distributed availability.
Apache Cassandra
Cassandra's own documentation describes it as AP by default, while also allowing consistency to be tuned per query. Its consistency level determines how many replicas must acknowledge an operation. For example, with replication factor 3, QUORUM requires two replicas. 
Imagine:
Replication Factor = 3

A
B
C
A write might use:
CL = ONE
or:
CL = QUORUM
or stronger/different consistency levels.
This means the simplistic statement:
"Cassandra = AP"
is useful as a broad architectural characterization, but incomplete.
________________________________________
10. Cassandra example
Suppose:
A = 10
B = 10
C = 10
Partition:
A ---- B       X       C
CL=ONE
A coordinator may be able to get one replica to acknowledge.
Potentially:
write -> success
even though the other side is unreachable.
That's availability-oriented behavior.
CL=QUORUM
For RF=3:
quorum = 2
The A/B side can satisfy:
2 replicas
The isolated C cannot.
Therefore:
A/B -> write can succeed
C   -> write cannot satisfy quorum
So Cassandra lets the application choose a consistency/availability trade-off per operation.
That's much more interesting than labeling the entire database "AP."
________________________________________
11. This gives us an important lesson
CAP isn't necessarily:
Database chooses:

CP
OR
AP
Real systems often expose:
consistency level
       ↓
per operation
       ↓
trade-off
For example:
Operation A:
QUORUM

Operation B:
ONE

Operation C:
LOCAL_QUORUM
Now your application's behavior depends on the actual consistency configuration.
This is why system-design answers like:
"Cassandra is AP."
are only the beginning of the discussion.
