12. Real system #2 — MongoDB
Now let's look at MongoDB.
MongoDB replica sets have:
Primary
   |
 +---+
 |   |
 S   S
Applications can configure:
•	write concern 
•	read concern 
•	read preference 
MongoDB explicitly documents that these settings let applications adjust consistency and availability guarantees. 
This is a fantastic real-world example because it destroys the simplistic:
"MongoDB is CP."
________________________________________
13. MongoDB write concern
Consider:
P
S1
S2
With:
w: 1
the primary can acknowledge after locally applying the write.
With:
w: "majority"
the write requires acknowledgment from a calculated majority of voting data-bearing members. In a typical 3-member replica set, that means 2 members. 
So:
w:1

P -> success
is different from:
w:majority

P + S1 -> success
The latter gives stronger durability/commit semantics.
________________________________________
14. MongoDB partition scenario
Suppose:
       P
      / \
     S1 S2
Partition:
P ---- S1       X       S2
Depending on the exact topology and election state, nodes may transiently believe different things about who is primary.
MongoDB's documentation explicitly discusses situations where two nodes may temporarily believe they are primary, but only one side can complete majority writes. Writes accepted with weaker w:1 semantics on the losing side can later roll back. 
This is an excellent example of a key distributed-systems concept:
A node believing it is primary is not the same thing as that node having a safely committed authority to perform writes.
________________________________________
15. MongoDB read concern
MongoDB supports several read concerns, including:
local
available
majority
snapshot
linearizable
The linearizable read concern specifically provides a real-time ordering guarantee for appropriate reads on the primary, while local can return data that is not majority-committed and may be rolled back. 
So you can conceptually have:
local
  ↓
less coordination
  ↓
more availability / lower latency
versus:
linearizable
  ↓
more coordination
  ↓
stronger guarantee
This is CAP theory appearing directly in an API.
________________________________________
16. Real system #3 — PostgreSQL
Now let's look at something many Java developers know extremely well:
PostgreSQL
Is PostgreSQL:
CP?
AP?
CA?
The answer is:
The question is incomplete.
PostgreSQL's built-in streaming replication is asynchronous by default. With asynchronous replication, a primary can commit data before the standby has received it, meaning a failover can lose transactions that had already committed on the primary. PostgreSQL also supports synchronous replication, where commits can wait for standby confirmation. 
So:
PostgreSQL replication
        |
        +---- asynchronous
        |
        +---- synchronous
These produce different behavior.
________________________________________
17. PostgreSQL asynchronous replication
Suppose:
Primary P
    |
    | WAL
    v
Standby S
Transaction:
COMMIT x=20
Primary returns success.
But:
P: x=20
S: x=10
for some time.
If P dies before S receives the WAL:
P -> DEAD
S -> promoted
then the committed transaction may be missing.
This is not a database bug.
It's the consequence of asynchronous replication.
PostgreSQL documents exactly this trade-off: asynchronous replication can expose stale reads and can lose transactions during failover; synchronous replication increases protection but adds waiting/latency. 
________________________________________
18. PostgreSQL synchronous replication
With synchronous replication:
Client
   |
   v
Primary
   |
   +----> Standby
            |
          ACK
            |
            v
        COMMIT OK
Now suppose:
Primary ----X---- Standby
What happens?
If the commit requires the standby:
COMMIT
   |
   v
WAIT
The transaction may not complete.
That's:
stronger durability
+
lower availability under failure
Again, CAP-like trade-offs appear.
PostgreSQL even supports quorum-based synchronous standby selection through settings such as synchronous_standby_names. 
________________________________________
19. Important correction: replication != consensus
PostgreSQL's physical streaming replication isn't automatically equivalent to a consensus protocol.
This distinction matters.
Replication answers:
"How do I copy state?"
Consensus answers:
"How do distributed nodes agree on one authoritative decision/history despite failures?"
These are related but different problems.
You can have:
replication
without:
consensus
And modern highly available PostgreSQL deployments often use external systems/tools for leader election and failover coordination.
This distinction becomes extremely important when you study:
•	Raft 
•	Paxos 
•	leader election 
•	fencing 
•	distributed locks 
________________________________________
20. Real system #4 — Redis
Now consider Redis.
Redis Cluster uses asynchronous replication by default. Redis explicitly states that Redis Cluster does not guarantee strong consistency and that acknowledged writes can be lost under certain failures, including partitions and failover scenarios. 
That means:
Client
   |
   v
Master B
   |
   | async
   v
Replica B1
The master can reply:
OK
before the replica has processed the write.
Then:
Master B crashes
and B1 becomes master.
If B1 never received the write:
write disappears
________________________________________
21. "But Redis has WAIT!"
Exactly.
Redis provides WAIT, which can request acknowledgment from a specified number of replicas.
But Redis explicitly notes that WAIT does not turn Redis into a strongly consistent CP system; writes can still be lost in certain failover scenarios. 
This is an excellent example of why:
"wait for replicas"
is not equivalent to:
"consensus"
or:
"linearizability"
________________________________________
22. Redis Cluster partition example
Imagine:
Masters:

A    B    C

Replicas:

A1   B1   C1
Partition:
Majority side:

A  C  A1  B1  C1

          X

Minority side:

B + client
The client connected to B might continue writing for some time.
Redis documents that if B's replica B1 gets promoted on the majority side after the relevant timeout, writes accepted by B during the partition can be lost. 
This is a beautiful real-world CAP scenario:
availability during partition
        ↓
accept writes
        ↓
possible divergent state
        ↓
failover
        ↓
some writes lost
________________________________________
23. Real system #5 — Kafka
Now let's use a system that is often incorrectly categorized in CAP discussions:
Apache Kafka
Kafka is primarily a distributed replicated log, not a general-purpose distributed database.
This matters.
A Kafka partition might have:
Leader
  |
  +---- Replica 1
  |
  +---- Replica 2
The replication semantics are based around:
ISR = in-sync replicas
and producer configuration such as:
acks
min.insync.replicas
Kafka's documentation says that with acks=all, min.insync.replicas can require a minimum number of in-sync replicas before a write succeeds. 
________________________________________
24. Kafka partition example
Suppose:
Replication Factor = 3

Leader A
Replica B
Replica C
and:
min.insync.replicas = 2
acks = all
Normal:
A B C
Write:
Producer -> A
Kafka can require sufficient ISR participation before considering the write successful.
Now partition:
A ---- B       X       C
If the leader side no longer has enough ISR members to satisfy:
min.insync.replicas = 2
the producer may receive an error rather than having Kafka silently accept the write. 
That's consistency/durability-oriented behavior at the log level.
________________________________________
25. Kafka is not simply "CP"
Kafka's own design documentation notes that it may remain available through node failures after failover, but may not remain available during network partitions. It also distinguishes producer acknowledgment choices and committed-message visibility. 
So saying:
"Kafka = CP"
is an oversimplification.
You need to ask:
•	Which Kafka version? 
•	Which replication configuration? 
•	What acks? 
•	What min.insync.replicas? 
•	What failure? 
•	What exactly do you mean by consistency? 
•	Are we talking about producer acknowledgment, consumer visibility, or global application semantics? 
Again:
CAP labels are not substitutes for understanding the protocol.
________________________________________
26. Real system #6 — Dynamo-style systems
Now let's go back to the system that historically influenced a lot of modern distributed database design:
Amazon's Dynamo architecture.
The original Dynamo design deliberately targeted:
high availability
+
partition tolerance
while allowing conflicts and eventual reconciliation.
The conceptual architecture looks like:
          Client
             |
             v
       +-----------+
       | Coordinator|
       +-----------+
        /    |    \
       v     v     v
      N1     N2    N3
During partition:
N1 N2       X       N3
the system can continue accepting operations rather than requiring a single globally coordinated history.
That architectural philosophy heavily influenced systems such as Cassandra.
________________________________________
27. Dynamo-style thinking
Suppose a shopping cart contains:
[]
During partition:
Region A:
add(book)

Region B:
add(phone)
Now:
A = [book]
B = [phone]
An availability-first system can eventually merge:
[book, phone]
This is very different from:
bank balance
where:
₹1000 - ₹700
cannot simply be independently executed on two sides.
Therefore:
CAP decisions are application-semantic decisions.
________________________________________
28. This gives us a powerful design principle
Don't ask:
"Should my entire system be CP or AP?"
Ask:
"Which operations require a single authoritative ordering, and which operations can tolerate divergence and reconciliation?"
For example:
Data	Likely requirement
Bank ledger	Strong coordination
Unique username	Strong coordination
Inventory reservation	Strong coordination
Shopping cart	Often mergeable
Like count	Often eventual
View count	Often eventual
Search index	Usually eventual
Cache	Usually eventual
Analytics	Usually eventual
This is much closer to real architecture.
________________________________________
29. Misconception #8 — "CAP applies to the whole database"
Not necessarily in the simplistic way people think.
A real distributed database can have:
operation 1 -> strong consistency
operation 2 -> eventual consistency
operation 3 -> causal consistency
operation 4 -> local read
depending on configuration and API.
Cassandra explicitly allows per-query consistency levels. 
MongoDB allows read/write concerns that change the guarantees. 
DynamoDB supports both eventually consistent and strongly consistent reads in a Region. 
So a system's marketing label doesn't tell you enough.
________________________________________
30. Misconception #9 — "Strong consistency means every replica has the same value"
No.
Imagine:
A = 20
B = 20
C = 10
C may simply be behind.
A linearizable read routed to C might need to:
wait
or:
route elsewhere
rather than expose 10.
So:
physical replica state
        ≠
logical consistency guarantee
This is a very important architectural distinction.
The system can internally have temporarily different states while externally providing strong semantics.
________________________________________
31. Misconception #10 — "Quorum means consistency"
Quorum is a mechanism, not a complete consistency guarantee.
Suppose:
N = 3

read quorum = 2
write quorum = 2
You get:
R + W > N
which means:
2 + 2 > 3
Therefore, read and write quorums intersect.
That's useful.
But whether that gives you linearizability depends on the complete protocol:
•	how versions are assigned 
•	how writes are ordered 
•	how conflicts are resolved 
•	how failures are handled 
•	what reads actually return 
•	whether stale replicas can answer 
•	whether there is a leader 
•	whether membership changes safely 
So:
quorum
   ≠
automatic linearizability
________________________________________
32. Quorum math you should know
For N replicas:
N = 5
A majority is:
3
A typical quorum relationship is:
R + W > N
For example:
R = 3
W = 3

3 + 3 > 5
Therefore, every read quorum intersects every write quorum.
But don't overgeneralize this formula.
It is useful in quorum-replication designs, but not the definition of CAP consistency.
________________________________________
33. Misconception #11 — "If I have 3 replicas, I'm highly available"
Not necessarily.
Suppose:
A
B
C
but all three are:
same availability zone
same rack
same power source
same network path
Then:
AZ failure
    ↓
A B C all disappear
Replication count isn't the same as failure-domain diversity.
A good distributed design asks:
What failure domain does each replica protect me against?
This is separate from CAP, but essential in real systems.
________________________________________
34. Misconception #12 — "Multi-region automatically means partition tolerant"
No.
Multi-region increases the possibility of surviving regional failures.
But your replication and coordination protocol determines what happens.
For example:
Region A -------- Region B
If the inter-region network fails:
Region A ----X---- Region B
you have a partition.
Now:
Can both regions accept writes?
If yes:
conflict risk
If no:
some clients become unavailable
Again:
CAP
________________________________________
35. Misconception #13 — "Consensus gives you availability"
Consensus solves a different problem.
Suppose:
A
B
C
and Raft requires a majority.
If:
A ---- B       X       C
A/B have 2/3.
Consensus can continue there.
But if:
A       X       B C
B/C have the majority.
A cannot commit new entries by itself.
If:
A B       X       C
A/B can continue.
But if:
A       X       B       X       C
and every node is isolated:
no majority
the protocol must stop committing new consensus decisions.
Therefore:
Consensus gives you agreement, but agreement itself requires enough participants to communicate.
This is why Raft-style systems are usually described as favoring consistency over availability under partition.
________________________________________
36. The majority principle behind Raft
A simplified Raft cluster:
Leader
  |
  +--- Follower
  |
  +--- Follower
For 3 nodes:
majority = 2
A log entry is safely committed only after sufficient replication/acknowledgment according to the protocol.
Partition:
Leader + Follower       X       Follower
Left side:
2 nodes
can maintain consensus.
Right side:
1 node
cannot independently commit new entries.
This is a very powerful mechanism because it prevents two disconnected sides from both safely committing conflicting histories.
________________________________________
37. Why "split brain" is so dangerous
Imagine two leaders:
        Partition

Leader A             Leader C
   |                     |
   v                     v
x=20                   x=30
Now:
Client 1 -> A
Client 2 -> C
Both receive:
SUCCESS
What is the authoritative history?
That's the split-brain problem.
Consensus protocols solve this by ensuring that only a node with sufficient authority/quorum can safely commit new decisions.
But there is a subtle distinction:
A node can temporarily believe it is leader without actually being able to commit a valid majority decision.
MongoDB's documentation gives a practical example of this exact phenomenon: two members can transiently believe they're primary, while only one side can complete majority writes. 
________________________________________
38. CAP vs consensus
Don't confuse:
CAP theorem
with:
Raft
Paxos
CAP says:
What guarantees cannot simultaneously be maintained under partition?
Consensus algorithms answer:
How can distributed nodes agree on one authoritative sequence/value despite failures?
Consensus is one of the tools used to build consistency-oriented distributed systems.
Conceptually:
CAP
 |
 | tells you the fundamental trade-off
 v
Consensus / quorum / replication protocols
 |
 | implement a chosen behavior
 v
Real system
________________________________________
39. CAP vs ACID
Another common interview mistake:
"CAP is the distributed version of ACID."
No.
They're talking about different things.
ACID
Primarily describes transaction properties:
Atomicity
Consistency
Isolation
Durability
CAP
Describes distributed operation guarantees under network partition:
Consistency
Availability
Partition tolerance
And the word "Consistency" means different things in these two contexts.
Especially:
CAP C ≠ ACID C
This is a very important interview point.
________________________________________
40. ACID's C is not CAP's C
In ACID, consistency means:
Transactions take the database from one valid state to another according to defined constraints/invariants.
For example:
balance >= 0
foreign key exists
unique username
CAP's C is about:
linearizable behavior across distributed replicas
These are completely different concepts.
So don't say:
"My PostgreSQL transaction is ACID consistent, therefore my distributed system satisfies CAP consistency."
That's mixing two meanings of consistency.
________________________________________
41. CAP vs PACELC
Now we're ready for another famous concept:
PACELC
The idea is:
If Partition:
    choose Availability or Consistency

Else:
    choose Latency or Consistency
Hence:
P A C
E L C
Conceptually:
          Partition?
          /       \
        YES        NO
        /           \
      A vs C       L vs C
This is useful because real systems have a trade-off even when there is no partition.
For example:
strong consistency
    ↓
remote coordination
    ↓
higher latency
versus:
local read
    ↓
lower latency
    ↓
possibly weaker consistency
PACELC is not a replacement for CAP, but it is a useful extension to architectural reasoning.
________________________________________
42. Example of PACELC
Suppose:
User in India

Region India
    |
    | 150 ms
    v
Region US
You want strong cross-region consistency.
Every operation might need:
India -> US
       |
       v
      ACK
Latency:
~150+ ms
If you instead allow local reads/writes:
India -> local replica
latency could be much lower.
So even when:
NO PARTITION
you still face:
Consistency vs Latency
This is why production distributed-system design goes beyond CAP.
________________________________________
43. Misconception #14 — "CAP tells me which database to choose"
No.
CAP is not a database-selection algorithm.
Suppose someone says:
"We're building an order system, so choose CP."
That isn't enough.
You need to ask:
What does an order mean?

Can duplicate orders exist?
Can conflicting inventory reservations exist?
Can stale reads be tolerated?
Can writes be retried?
Can operations be reconciled?
Does payment require strict ordering?
Then choose protocols and storage semantics.
CAP helps you reason about the trade-offs.
It doesn't choose your architecture automatically.
________________________________________
