48. The "ACK" is more important than it looks
As a backend developer, you should become suspicious whenever you see:
200 OK
in a distributed system.
Ask:
What exactly does this ACK mean?
For example:
Redis
OK
might mean:
master accepted the write
not:
multiple replicas durably committed it
Redis documents its asynchronous replication and the limitations of WAIT. 
MongoDB
write acknowledged
can mean different things depending on:
w:1
w:majority
Kafka
acks=all
has different implications from:
acks=1
and min.insync.replicas further constrains when a write can succeed. 
So:
An ACK is a statement about what the system promises, not merely a network response.
This is a very valuable distributed-systems habit.
