49. Another misconception — "Timeout means operation failed"
No.
Suppose:
Client -> Server
           |
           v
       commit write
           |
           X
       response lost
Client sees:
timeout
But the operation may have succeeded.
This means:
timeout
   ≠
failure
It means:
The client did not obtain the expected response in time.
This is why distributed systems need:
•	idempotency keys 
•	request IDs 
•	deduplication 
•	transactional outbox 
•	exactly-once-ish processing strategies 
•	retry-safe APIs 
CAP doesn't directly solve these problems, but the same distributed uncertainty causes them.
