50. "Exactly once" is another trap
Suppose:
POST /payment
Client sends it.
Server commits.
Response lost.
Client retries.
Now you have:
same logical operation
sent twice
You cannot simply say:
"TCP guarantees exactly once."
TCP gives you reliable ordered bytes on a connection.
It does not give you:
business operation exactly once
across:
•	network failure 
•	server crash 
•	retry 
•	transaction commit 
•	response loss 
Again:
network semantics
    ≠
application semantics
This is a major distributed-systems lesson.
