52. Your interview-quality explanation of CAP
If I were evaluating you in a system-design interview, this would be a strong answer:
CAP states that in a distributed system, when a network partition prevents replicas from communicating, you cannot simultaneously guarantee linearizability and availability. A consistency-oriented system may refuse or delay operations that cannot be safely coordinated, while an availability-oriented system may continue serving requests and reconcile divergent state later. Partition tolerance isn't really an optional feature for systems operating over unreliable networks; the important design decision is what guarantees the system sacrifices during a partition.
Then give this example:
A -------- B       X       C

A commits x=20.

C receives READ x.

If C returns 10:
    available
    but not linearizable.

If C refuses:
    linearizability preserved
    but unavailable.
That demonstrates actual understanding.
