14. Idempotency enters here
A function is idempotent if:
f(f(x)) = f(x)
Conceptually.
For example:
SET account.status = "ACTIVE"
Repeated execution:
ACTIVE → ACTIVE → ACTIVE
has the same final effect.
But:
balance = balance - 100
is not idempotent.
Repeated execution:
1000
 ↓ -100
900
 ↓ -100
800
changes the result.
Therefore:
SET status = ACTIVE
is naturally idempotent.
Whereas:
balance -= 100
is not.
This difference becomes central to designing distributed systems.
