Kafka offset commit
   ↓
CRASH
If the crash occurs between those two operations:
DB = committed
Kafka offset = not committed
After restart:
message redelivered
So:
DB operation
runs again.
This is the famous dual-write problem.
You are trying to atomically update:
System A = database
System B = Kafka offset
but they don't share one atomic transaction.
________________________________________
22. The dual-write diagram
This is worth memorizing:
             Java Consumer
                 |
        ┌────────┴────────┐
        ↓                 ↓
   PostgreSQL          Kafka
   DB state            Offset
        |                 |
     COMMIT             COMMIT
        |                 |
        └────── ??? ──────┘
There is always potentially a failure window between them.
DB first
DB commit
   ↓
CRASH
   ↓
Kafka offset not committed
   ↓
retry
Potential duplicate.
Kafka first
Kafka offset commit
   ↓
CRASH
   ↓
DB transaction not committed
Potential loss.
Same fundamental dilemma.
________________________________________
23. This is why "exactly once processing" needs atomicity
If you want:
process message
+
advance message position
to behave atomically, you need some mechanism to tie them together.
Conceptually:
┌───────────────────────────────┐
│       ONE ATOMIC UNIT         │
│                               │
│  process message              │
│       +                       │
│  record completion            │
│       +                       │
│  advance position             │
│                               │
└───────────────────────────────┘
Kafka transactions can create a version of this within Kafka's transactional model.
That's why Lesson 3 will focus heavily on:
What Kafka transactions actually make atomic—and what they don't.
________________________________________
24. Duplicate execution and concurrency
There's another category that's not simply "retry."
Suppose:
M123
is accidentally processed concurrently by two consumers/services:
Consumer A ──→ process(M123)
Consumer B ──→ process(M123)
This can happen due to:
•	application bugs 
•	overlapping retry mechanisms 
•	incorrect partitioning assumptions 
•	stale consumers 
•	external redelivery 
•	race conditions 
Now we have:
concurrent duplicate execution
This is more dangerous than sequential duplication.
Sequential:
A
↓
A again
Concurrent:
A ──────────→
             \
              shared state
             /
B ──────────→
Now the two executions can interfere with each other.
________________________________________
25. Duplicate execution vs race condition
Suppose both executions do:
UPDATE account
SET balance = balance - 1000
WHERE id = 10;
Even if the DB handles concurrency correctly, the operation may happen twice.
But suppose they do:
read balance
calculate new balance
write balance
Then you can get a separate race condition:
A reads 10000
B reads 10000

A writes 9000
B writes 9000
Now one operation may effectively disappear.
So distributed systems have multiple layers of problems:
duplicate execution
        +
concurrent execution
        +
race conditions
        +
partial failure
Don't collapse them into one concept.
________________________________________
26. Duplicate execution is not always bad
This is a subtle but extremely important engineering principle.
Suppose:
process(M123)
runs three times:
execution #1
execution #2
execution #3
but all three execute:
INSERT INTO processed_messages(message_id)
VALUES ('M123');
with:
UNIQUE(message_id)
Then:
execution:
3

business effect:
1
This system may be perfectly acceptable.
In fact, many highly reliable systems intentionally use:
At-least-once execution + idempotent effects
instead of trying to guarantee literal exactly-once execution.
This is one of the most important practical lessons in distributed systems.
________________________________________
27. A useful equation
Think about the architecture like this:
At-least-once delivery
        +
possible duplicate execution
        +
idempotent business effect
        =
effectively exactly-once behavior
Not universally.
But very often.
And this is usually much easier to build and reason about than:
every layer must execute exactly once
________________________________________
28. Example: order creation
Suppose:
Order ID = O123
Consumer does:
INSERT INTO orders(order_id, customer_id)
VALUES ('O123', 42);
with:
PRIMARY KEY(order_id)
First execution:
O123 inserted
Second execution:
duplicate key
Now the second execution can be treated as:
already completed
The important thing is that the business identity is stable.
Don't deduplicate based on:
timestamp
or:
Java object identity
Use a durable business/operation identifier.
________________________________________
29. Example: increment is much harder
Compare:
UPDATE account
SET balance = balance - 1000
WHERE id = 10;
with:
UPDATE account
SET last_payment_id = 'P123',
    balance = balance - 1000
WHERE id = 10
  AND last_payment_id <> 'P123';
Now the operation carries an identity:
P123
The database can reason:
"Have I already applied payment P123?"
This is the beginning of an idempotent state transition.
In real systems, you'd generally model this more carefully than this simplified SQL, but the conceptual move is what's important.
________________________________________
30. The four states you should reason about
For every message, ask:
Did we receive it?
Did we execute it?
Did the business transaction commit?
Did we record completion?
For example:
Receive       = YES
Execute       = YES
DB commit     = YES
Offset commit = NO
Then:
redelivery expected
Another:
Receive       = YES
Execute       = YES
DB commit     = NO
Offset commit = YES
Potential:
lost business effect
Another:
Receive       = YES
Execute       = YES
DB commit     = YES
Completion    = YES
Offset commit = YES
Ideal.
This way of thinking is much more powerful than simply saying:
"Kafka is at least once."
________________________________________
31. A production debugging technique
When someone tells you:
"We're getting duplicate messages."
Don't immediately change Kafka configuration.
Ask these questions:
Question 1
Is the Kafka record actually duplicated?
Check:
topic
partition
offset
key
message ID
Question 2
Or is the same offset being processed multiple times?
Look at:
consumer logs
rebalance events
consumer group state
Question 3
Where exactly did duplicate execution happen?
Kafka
 ↓
Java method
 ↓
DB
 ↓
external API
Question 4
Was the first execution actually committed?
Question 5
Did a timeout cause a retry?
Question 6
Was there a rebalance?
Question 7
Are multiple retry mechanisms active?
Only after answering these should you decide whether Kafka itself is involved.
________________________________________
32. A distributed-systems timeline worth memorizing
Here is one of the most important sequences:
                 Consumer
                    |
Kafka ── M123 ────→|
                    |
                    | process
                    |
                    | DB COMMIT
                    |
                    X CRASH
                    |
                    |
Kafka: "offset not committed"
                    |
                    |
             restart consumer
                    |
Kafka ── M123 ────→|
                    |
                    | process AGAIN
                    |
This is not an exceptional corner case.
It is the natural consequence of:
database commit
        ≠
Kafka offset commit
unless you have a mechanism that atomically coordinates the relevant state.
________________________________________
33. The deeper principle: "Exactly once" tries to eliminate history ambiguity
At the end of Lesson 1, we said:
After failure, we don't know whether the operation happened.
Duplicate execution is what happens when the system chooses:
"Let's retry."
without knowing whether the first attempt succeeded.
So the engineering question becomes:
How can we make retry safe?
There are several approaches:
             Retry safety
                  |
       ┌──────────┼───────────┐
       ↓          ↓           ↓
  idempotency  deduplication  transactions
       |          |           |
       └──────────┼───────────┘
                  ↓
        exactly-once effects
And now we're ready for Kafka transactions.
