38. Lesson 3 — What you should be able to explain now
If someone asks:
"What does Kafka's idempotent producer solve?"
You should answer:
It prevents certain duplicate record appends caused by producer retries by using producer identity and sequence numbers.
"What does a Kafka transaction solve?"
It allows multiple Kafka operations, particularly output records and consumer-offset commits, to be committed atomically.
"Why do I need read_committed?"
So consumers don't treat records from aborted Kafka transactions as committed business output.
"What is producer fencing?"
Kafka prevents stale producers sharing an old transactional identity from continuing to write once a newer producer epoch has taken ownership.
"Does Kafka exactly-once mean my database is exactly once?"
No. Kafka transactions don't automatically include arbitrary external systems.
