34. Outbox solves the local atomicity problem
Instead:
BEGIN DB TRANSACTION

UPDATE orders

INSERT INTO outbox_events(...)

COMMIT
Now:
┌───────────────────────────┐
│      PostgreSQL TX        │
│                           │
│ update order              │
│                           │
│ insert outbox event       │
│                           │
└───────────────────────────┘
Both are atomic.
Then a separate publisher reads:
outbox_events
and publishes to Kafka.
Architecture:
             PostgreSQL
          ┌───────────────┐
          │ orders        │
          │ outbox_events │
          └───────┬───────┘
                  │
                  ↓
             Outbox relay
                  │
                  ↓
                Kafka
This does not magically give exactly-once Kafka publishing, but it creates a durable source from which publishing can be retried safely.
________________________________________
35. Outbox + idempotency
A very common production architecture becomes:
Incoming message
       ↓
DB transaction
       |
       +── business state
       |
       +── inbox/idempotency record
       |
       +── outbox event
       |
      COMMIT
       ↓
Outbox relay
       ↓
Kafka
Now the database is the authoritative local transaction boundary.
This is an extremely powerful pattern.
________________________________________
