42. State machines are often better than "exactly once"
Consider:
P123

REQUESTED
   ↓
PROCESSING
   ↓
SUCCEEDED
Suppose the worker crashes after calling the payment provider.
You may not know whether the provider succeeded.
Instead of blindly charging again, you can ask:
What is the status of P123?
This is a different strategy:
Make operations recoverable and queryable instead of pretending failures don't happen.
Distributed systems often need reconciliation, not just transactions.
________________________________________
43. Exactly-once effects don't eliminate uncertainty
This is subtle.
Suppose:
payment API
times out.
You still don't know:
charged?
not charged?
Idempotency doesn't necessarily make the answer immediately knowable.
What it gives you is:
retry with same P123
without causing another charge.
So:
uncertainty
    +
safe retry
    =
recoverability
This is a much more realistic goal than trying to eliminate uncertainty.
