    markProcessed(command.paymentId());
}
But remember:
The actual implementation should not rely on a simple pre-check for concurrency.
Prefer the database to enforce uniqueness:
INSERT P123
with:
UNIQUE(payment_id)
and handle the duplicate atomically.
The database constraint is the real concurrency guard.
________________________________________
45. What if processing takes a long time?
Suppose:
BEGIN
INSERT P123
   ↓
long processing
   ↓
COMMIT
Now you've held a DB transaction open for a long time.
That may be problematic.
This leads to an important design rule:
Don't casually put slow network calls inside a database transaction just because you want idempotency.
For example:
BEGIN DB TX
   ↓
call external API
   ↓
wait 30 sec
   ↓
COMMIT
can create:
•	long locks 
•	connection exhaustion 
•	contention 
•	deadlocks 
•	poor throughput 
Instead, design an explicit workflow/state machine where possible.
________________________________________
46. Idempotency and concurrency are different dimensions
Consider:
P123
received:
sequentially
versus:
concurrently
An idempotent design must handle both.
Sequential:
P123
 ↓
P123
Concurrent:
P123 ──→ Worker A
P123 ──→ Worker B
The database uniqueness constraint/atomic transaction must protect the second case.
So a good idempotency design needs:
retry safety
+
concurrency safety
+
crash safety
________________________________________
47. The three failure scenarios to test
For every idempotent operation, explicitly test:
Failure 1
before business effect
Failure 2
after business effect
but before acknowledgement
Failure 3
after acknowledgement
The most important is #2.
Example:
business effect
      ↓
      X crash
      ↓
retry
If the system handles this correctly:
business effect = once
you've solved the core problem.
________________________________________
48. The "crash injection" mindset
When designing distributed systems, don't ask only:
"What happens when everything works?"
Ask:
"What if the process dies at every line between two durable state changes?"
For example:
receive
  ↓
validate
  ↓
DB insert
  ↓
DB commit
  ↓
Kafka commit
  ↓
response
Potential crash points:
after receive
after validate
after DB insert
after DB commit
before Kafka commit
after Kafka commit
before response
Each boundary needs a defined recovery behavior.
This is how you reason like a distributed-systems engineer rather than an application developer.
________________________________________
49. Exactly-once effects as a state-machine property
Here's a deeper formulation.
Suppose operation:
P123
has states:
UNKNOWN
   ↓
PROCESSING
   ↓
COMPLETED
We want retries to converge toward:
COMPLETED
rather than repeatedly applying:
PROCESS
PROCESS
PROCESS
So idempotency is really about creating a state transition:
P123:
UNKNOWN → COMPLETED
that is safe to attempt repeatedly.
Repeated attempts should converge to the same state.
________________________________________
50. This is why immutable operation IDs are powerful
Instead of:
"subtract ₹1000"
think:
"apply operation P123"
Now the system can ask:
Has P123 been applied?
That question is answerable.
Without the operation identity:
"subtract ₹1000"
there may be no way to distinguish:
new payment
from:
retry
So:
Stable identity converts an ambiguous retry into a recognizable retry.
This is one of the deepest ideas in reliable distributed processing.
________________________________________
51. Putting Lessons 1–4 together
We now have:
                   Failure
                      ↓
                 uncertainty
                      ↓
                    retry
                      ↓
             duplicate execution
                      ↓
            ┌─────────┴─────────┐
            ↓                   ↓
