Final takeaway
The deepest lesson of the entire series is this:
Distributed systems don't become reliable because you eliminate retries. They become reliable because retries become safe.
And:
exactly-once execution
        ↓
usually unrealistic globally

exactly-once business effect
        ↓
often achievable
        ↓
stable identity
+
atomicity
+
idempotency
+
deduplication
+
durable state
+
reconciliation
So when you design distributed systems, don't ask only:
"How do I execute this exactly once?"
Ask the more useful question:
"If this executes 0, 1, 2, or 10 times because of failures, how do I guarantee that the final business state is correct?"
That is the real distributed-systems mindset.

At-Least-Once Delivery — Lesson 1: Delivery Semantics
1. First: what problem are we actually solving?
Imagine:
Producer
   |
   |  OrderCreated(orderId=123)
   v
Message Broker
   |
   v
Consumer
The producer wants to communicate:
"Please process OrderCreated(123)."
The difficult part isn't sending the message.
The difficult part is this:
How do we know whether the consumer actually processed it when failures can happen between any two steps?
That question leads directly to delivery semantics.
________________________________________
2. Delivery semantics
There are three classical models:
At-most-once
At-least-once
Exactly-once
Think about the number of times the consumer can observe/process a message.
Semantic	Possible delivery count
At-most-once	0 or 1
At-least-once	1 or more
Exactly-once	exactly 1
But that table hides an extremely important distinction.
Delivery is not necessarily processing
Suppose the broker sends:
Message M
   |
   v
Consumer
The broker may consider M "delivered" because it successfully transmitted it.
But the consumer may not have completed its business operation.
So when discussing delivery semantics, you should ask:
At which boundary are we defining "delivered"?
This is one of the most important concepts in distributed systems.
________________________________________
3. The fundamental failure problem
Consider a consumer:
void consume(Message message) {
    process(message);
    acknowledge(message);
}
Suppose:
        Consumer

1. receive message
2. process business operation
3. acknowledge broker
Looks straightforward.
Now introduce a crash.
Timeline
Broker                    Consumer
  |                          |
  |---- OrderCreated ------->|
  |                          |
  |                     process()
  |                          |
  |                  DB update succeeds
  |                          |
  |                     💥 CRASH
  |                          |
  |                    acknowledge()
  |                    never happens
When the consumer comes back:
Broker
  |
  | "I never received the ACK."
  |
  |---- OrderCreated -------> Consumer
The message is processed again.
So:
Business operation
     ↓
success
     ↓
consumer crashes
     ↓
message redelivered
     ↓
business operation again
That's the heart of at-least-once delivery.
