5. The impossible window
Let's make it more formal.
We have two important operations:
P = business processing
A = acknowledgment
We want:
P succeeds
AND
A succeeds
But these are separate operations.
There is always some transition:
P
|
|   failure can happen here
|
A
If:
P succeeds
A fails
we get a duplicate.
If:
A succeeds
P fails
we get a lost message.
This is why at-least-once delivery naturally favors ACK-after-processing.
receive
   ↓
process
   ↓
ACK
If the ACK is lost, the broker retries.
Therefore:
processed 1 time
processed 2 times
processed 3 times
...
But importantly:
The system tries to ensure the message isn't permanently lost, at the cost of potentially processing it multiple times.
That's the central trade-off.
