7. Sequential vs concurrent duplicates
Sequential duplicate
Consumer

M
↓
process
↓
M
↓
detect duplicate
Usually easier to handle.
Concurrent duplicate
             M
             |
        ┌────┴────┐
        ↓         ↓
    Consumer A  Consumer B
        |         |
        ↓         ↓
     process   process
Both consumers might ask:
"Has M already been processed?"
at almost exactly the same time.
Both get:
NO
Both proceed.
And you've discovered one of the most important problems in deduplication:
A check followed by an action is not automatically atomic.
________________________________________
8. The classic broken idempotency implementation
A junior implementation might be:
if (!processedEvents.contains(eventId)) {
    process(event);
    processedEvents.add(eventId);
}
Looks reasonable.
But imagine two consumer threads:
Thread A                  Thread B

contains(M) = false       contains(M) = false

process(M)                process(M)

add(M)                    add(M)
Business operation happens twice.
So:
check
 ↓
process
 ↓
record
is unsafe under concurrency.
This is a huge concept.
