9. Why an in-memory Set is not enough
You might try:
private final Set<String> processed =
    ConcurrentHashMap.newKeySet();
Better than a normal HashSet, but still insufficient for a distributed consumer.
Why?
Because you may have:
Consumer instance A
Consumer instance B
Consumer instance C
Each has its own memory.
             Broker
            /      \
           /        \
          ↓          ↓
     Instance A   Instance B
        Set A        Set B
A processes:
M
and records:
Set A = {M}
Then B receives M:
Set B = {}
B doesn't know A processed it.
So distributed deduplication generally requires shared durable state.
________________________________________
10. Database-backed deduplication
A common pattern is:
processed_messages

----------------------------
message_id       processed_at
----------------------------
abc-123          ...
abc-456          ...
Consumer:
receive M
   ↓
check processed_messages
   ↓
already exists?
   ├── yes → ignore
   └── no  → process
But we've just encountered the race condition again.
Two consumers:
A: SELECT ... WHERE id = M → none
B: SELECT ... WHERE id = M → none
Both process.
So the solution isn't merely:
"Use a database."
The important question is:
How do we make the deduplication decision atomic?
