12. But there's still a major trap
Suppose you do:
if (insertProcessedMessage(eventId)) {
    process(event);
}
Imagine:
INSERT M
   ↓
SUCCESS
   ↓
💥 CRASH
   ↓
process(M) never happens
Now the database says:
M = processed
but the business operation never happened.
You've created message loss.
This is why the ordering matters enormously.
________________________________________
13. The better transactional structure
Suppose both:
processed_messages
and:
orders
are in the same database.
Then you can do:
@Transactional
public void consume(OrderCreated event) {

    insertProcessedMessage(event.id());

    orderRepository.create(event.orderId());

    // transaction commits
}
Now:
INSERT processed message
+
business operation
are in the same database transaction.
Either:
both commit
or:
both rollback
That's dramatically safer.
________________________________________
14. The transaction timeline
First delivery:
BEGIN
  |
  +-- INSERT processed_messages(M)
  |
  +-- INSERT order(123)
  |
COMMIT
Everything succeeds.
Later duplicate:
BEGIN
  |
  +-- INSERT processed_messages(M)
       |
       X duplicate key
  |
ROLLBACK
Consumer knows:
M already processed
and can safely ignore it.
Conceptually:
              M
              |
              v
      ┌──────────────────┐
      │ DB transaction   │
      │                  │
      │ dedup record     │
      │       +          │
      │ business effect  │
      └────────┬─────────┘
               |
             COMMIT
This is one of the most useful patterns for at-least-once consumers.
________________________________________
15. But message ID is a very important design decision
Consider:
{
  "messageId": "abc-123",
  "orderId": "42"
}
What does messageId mean?
Possibility:
messageId = unique delivery attempt
or:
messageId = unique logical event
Those are very different.
Suppose producer retries:
OrderCreated
messageId=A
fails to get a response.
Producer sends again:
OrderCreated
messageId=B
Now:
A != B
but semantically:
same OrderCreated event
Your deduplication based only on message ID won't detect it.
This gives us an important concept:
Idempotency identity must match the business semantics you want to protect.
________________________________________
16. Transport identity vs business identity
Consider:
Transport ID:
messageId = 8f21

Business ID:
orderId = 123
eventType = OrderCreated
You may need to reason about:
(messageId)
versus:
(orderId, eventType)
versus:
(orderId, operation)
depending on the domain.
For example:
OrderCreated(123)
should probably happen once.
But:
OrderUpdated(123)
OrderUpdated(123)
may represent two legitimate updates if they have different versions.
So blindly saying:
"Deduplicate by orderId."
could be catastrophically wrong.
________________________________________
17. Example: why business-key deduplication can be dangerous
Imagine:
OrderStatusChanged
orderId = 100
status = PACKED
Then:
OrderStatusChanged
orderId = 100
status = SHIPPED
Same:
orderId = 100
but these are different events.
If you deduplicate only by:
orderId
you might discard:
SHIPPED
because:
100 was already seen
Wrong.
You might instead need:
eventId
or:
(orderId, version)
or another domain-specific identity.
This is why idempotency is not merely a technical concern.
It's partly a domain modeling problem.
________________________________________
18. Duplicate does not always mean identical payload
This is an advanced but important case.
Suppose:
messageId = X
arrives twice.
First:
{
  "messageId": "X",
  "orderId": 123,
  "status": "PAID"
}
Second:
{
  "messageId": "X",
  "orderId": 123,
  "status": "CANCELLED"
}
That indicates a producer/data-integrity problem.
A robust system shouldn't silently say:
"X already exists, ignore."
It may need to detect:
same ID
different payload
because that can indicate corruption, producer bugs, or identity misuse.
________________________________________
19. Redelivery can happen after partial business work
This is where things get much more interesting.
Suppose:
public void process(OrderCreated event) {

    orderRepository.create(event);

    emailService.sendOrderConfirmation(event);

    paymentService.reserve(event);
}
And:
create order
   ↓
email sent
   ↓
💥 crash
   ↓
payment not reserved
Redelivery gives:
create order again
   ↓
email again
   ↓
payment again
Now you have three different side effects with three different duplication characteristics.
This is why:
"Make the consumer idempotent" is not one generic operation.
You have to examine every side effect.
________________________________________
20. Side effects fall into different categories
Database write
Usually easiest to make idempotent.
Example:
INSERT ... ON CONFLICT ...
or:
unique constraint
or:
upsert
________________________________________
Sending an email
Harder.
You don't control the recipient's inbox transactionally.
DB transaction
      |
      X
Email provider
You need another mechanism, such as:
idempotency key
if the provider supports it, or an outbox/workflow design.
________________________________________
Charging a payment
Much more serious.
You absolutely don't want:
retry
 ↓
second charge
So you typically use a business idempotency key:
paymentRequestId = orderId + paymentAttemptId
and the payment service must guarantee:
same idempotency key
       ↓
same logical operation
       ↓
no duplicate charge
________________________________________
21. A very useful classification
When analyzing a consumer, make a table mentally:
Operation	Duplicate consequence	Typical protection
DB update	incorrect state	transaction/constraint
Increment counter	double count	idempotent update / event identity
Send email	duplicate email	provider idempotency/outbox
Charge card	double charge	idempotency key
Publish event	duplicate downstream event	transactional outbox / dedup
Call external API	repeated side effect	idempotency key
This is how you should approach system design.
Don't ask merely:
"Is my consumer idempotent?"
Ask:
"Which side effects can repeat, and what mechanism makes each one safe?"
________________________________________
22. A subtle issue: retries can amplify duplicates
Suppose:
Broker
  ↓
Consumer
  ↓
External API
External API takes 5 seconds.
Consumer timeout:
2 seconds
So:
attempt 1
   ↓
API request sent
   ↓
consumer timeout
   ↓
retry
   ↓
API request sent again
But the first request may still be executing.
Now:
API
├── request #1
└── request #2
Both may succeed.
This is the classic:
timeout does not imply failure
problem.
Your retry mechanism can actually create the duplicate.
________________________________________
23. Retry storms
Imagine:
10,000 messages
and a downstream service fails.
Consumers retry aggressively:
M1 → retry
M2 → retry
M3 → retry
...
M10000 → retry
The downstream system recovers briefly and gets hammered again.
This is a retry storm.
So safe at-least-once systems typically need:
exponential backoff
+
jitter
+
retry limits
+
dead-letter handling
+
circuit breaking where appropriate
This is not just performance engineering.
It affects correctness and system stability.
________________________________________
24. Redelivery + ordering
Duplicates become even more complicated when ordering matters.
Suppose:
M1 = balance = 100
M2 = balance = 50
Correct order:
M1 → M2
But redelivery can produce:
M1 → M2 → M1
If your consumer simply processes everything:
balance = 100
You have reverted state.
So an idempotent consumer may also need:
version checking
For example:
event version = 42
current version = 43
Then:
ignore event 42
This is a different form of duplicate/out-of-order protection.
________________________________________
25. Idempotency vs deduplication
These terms are often used interchangeably, but they're not exactly the same.
Deduplication
Means:
Detect that I've already seen this event.
Example:
messageId = X
already exists.
So:
ignore
________________________________________
Idempotency
Means:
Performing the operation multiple times produces the same intended business result as performing it once.
Example:
set status = PAID
twice:
PAID
PAID
Final state is still:
PAID
That's naturally idempotent.
But:
balance = balance + 100
is not naturally idempotent.
Two executions produce:
+200
instead of:
+100
So you may need deduplication or another mechanism.
________________________________________
26. A beautiful example of natural idempotency
Compare:
UPDATE orders
SET status = 'PAID'
WHERE order_id = 123;
Repeated:
PAID
PAID
PAID
Final result:
PAID
versus:
UPDATE accounts
SET balance = balance + 100
WHERE account_id = 123;
Repeated:
+100
+100
+100
Result:
+300
So when designing consumers, ask:
Can I formulate the business operation as an idempotent state assignment rather than a non-idempotent increment?
Sometimes that simplifies the architecture dramatically.
________________________________________
27. But "set" operations aren't automatically safe
Suppose:
M1: status = PAID
M2: status = CANCELLED
If M1 is redelivered after M2:
M1
M2
M1
you end at:
PAID
even though the correct latest state is:
CANCELLED
So idempotency alone doesn't solve:
ordering
You may need:
event version
Example:
M1 version=10
M2 version=11
Consumer:
if incomingVersion <= currentVersion:
    ignore
Now:
version 10
version 11
version 10
becomes:
10 → 11 → ignore 10
This is version-based stale-event protection.
________________________________________
28. The consumer is becoming a state machine
At this point, stop thinking of your consumer as:
void consume(Message m)
Think:
                 Message
                    |
                    v
             ┌──────────────┐
             │ identify event│
             └──────┬───────┘
                    |
                    v
             ┌──────────────┐
             │ duplicate?   │
             └──────┬───────┘
                yes / \ no
                   /   \
                ignore  |
                        v
                ┌─────────────┐
                │ valid order?│
                └──────┬──────┘
                       |
                       v
                business logic
                       |
                       v
                  commit state
The consumer is a state transition engine operating under repeated inputs and failures.
That is a much better mental model.
________________________________________
29. A Java example: naïve consumer
public void consume(OrderCreated event) {

    if (orderRepository.exists(event.orderId())) {
        return;
    }

    orderRepository.create(event);
}
Looks idempotent.
But concurrent consumers:
Consumer A              Consumer B

exists(123) = false     exists(123) = false

create(123)             create(123)
Both may succeed unless the database protects the invariant.
Therefore:
Application-level checks are not enough when concurrency exists.
Push invariants into the database when appropriate.
________________________________________
30. Better Java design
Suppose:
CREATE UNIQUE INDEX ux_orders_order_id
ON orders(order_id);
Then:
public void consume(OrderCreated event) {

    try {
        orderRepository.create(event);
    } catch (DuplicateKeyException e) {
        // already processed
    }
}
Now the database establishes:
Only one order 123 can exist.
That's stronger than:
if (!exists()) {
    create();
}
because the database's uniqueness guarantee is atomic.
________________________________________
31. But what if the message has multiple business effects?
Suppose:
OrderCreated
   ↓
create order
   +
reserve inventory
   +
publish OrderReady
Now one unique constraint isn't enough.
You need to reason about:
        OrderCreated
             |
       ┌─────┼─────┐
       ↓     ↓     ↓
     order inventory event
If processing crashes after the first two:
order = created
inventory = reserved
event = not published
Redelivery must not:
create order again
reserve inventory again
and somehow still needs to eventually publish the event.
This is where transactional outbox becomes extremely useful.
________________________________________
32. The deeper pattern emerging
For database-backed consumers:
Message
   ↓
DB transaction
   ├── deduplication record
   ├── business state change
   └── outgoing event/outbox
   ↓
COMMIT
Then an independent publisher sends the outbox event.
This gives you a much cleaner boundary:
Consumer
   ↓
Database transaction
   ↓
durable state
   ↓
Outbox publisher
   ↓
Broker
Instead of trying to atomically coordinate:
Database + Broker + External API
with one giant distributed transaction.
We'll revisit this in Lesson 3.
________________________________________
33. Duplicate delivery is not always a problem
This is an important mindset shift.
Suppose your consumer is:
receive OrderCreated
      ↓
INSERT order
      ↓
unique(orderId)
First:
M → create order
Second:
M → duplicate key → ignore
Third:
M → duplicate key → ignore
Then:
10 deliveries
can still produce:
1 business effect
That's exactly what we want.
So the goal isn't:
"Prevent duplicate delivery."
The goal is:
"Make duplicate delivery harmless."
This is a major architectural principle.
You often cannot reliably prevent duplicates.
You design for them.
________________________________________
34. Three layers of duplicate protection
A mature system can have several layers.
Layer 1 — Broker
Attempts to deliver:
at least once
Layer 2 — Consumer
Detects repeated messages:
deduplication
Layer 3 — Business/database
Enforces invariants:
unique order
unique payment
version ordering
So even if the consumer makes a mistake, the database can protect important business invariants.
Think:
Broker guarantee
       ↓
Consumer logic
       ↓
Database invariant
Defense in depth.
________________________________________
35. The most dangerous duplicate: external side effects
Here's the scenario I want you to remember.
Consumer
   |
   | POST /charge
   v
Payment Service
   |
   | SUCCESS
   v
Consumer
   |
   | 💥 crash
   X
Broker redelivers.
Consumer:
POST /charge
again.
Payment service:
charge again
Now you have:
₹100
₹100
charged twice.
A local database unique constraint cannot directly protect you because:
Consumer DB
and:
Payment DB
are different transactional domains.
This is where idempotency keys at service boundaries become crucial.
________________________________________
36. The payment idempotency pattern
Consumer generates or receives:
idempotencyKey = paymentOperationId
Then:
POST /payments
Idempotency-Key: abc-123
First request:
abc-123
→ charge ₹100
→ success
Retry:
abc-123
→ already processed
→ return previous result
So:
request #1
request #2
request #3
all represent:
one logical operation
This is the same core idea as message deduplication, but applied across a service boundary.
