Lesson 2 → Lesson 3 bridge

At-Least-Once — Lesson 3: Designing Consumers Safely
This is the most important lesson of the three.
Lesson 1 gave us:
At-least-once
    ↓
ACK can be lost
    ↓
redelivery possible
Lesson 2 gave us:
redelivery
    ↓
duplicate processing
    ↓
idempotency / deduplication required
Now Lesson 3 asks:
How do I actually build a consumer that remains correct when messages are duplicated, retried, reordered, delayed, or when the JVM dies at the worst possible moment?
We'll build the answer from first principles.
________________________________________
1. First principle: assume the message will arrive again
When designing an at-least-once consumer, adopt this assumption:
Every message may arrive:

M
M
M
M
M
...
And not just eventually.
It may arrive:
M
M concurrently
on two different consumer instances.
Therefore, this:
public void consume(OrderCreated event) {
    createOrder(event);
}
is not enough.
The real requirement is:
Repeated execution of the same logical event must not violate business invariants.
________________________________________
2. Start with a naïve consumer
Imagine:
public void consume(OrderCreated event) {

    orderRepository.create(
        event.orderId(),
        event.customerId()
    );

    acknowledge(event);
}
Execution:
receive
   ↓
create order
   ↓
ACK
Looks fine.
But now inject a crash:
receive
   ↓
create order
   ↓
COMMIT
   ↓
💥 JVM dies
   ↓
ACK never happens
Broker:
redeliver
Second execution:
create order
Potential result:
duplicate order
So we need an invariant:
OrderCreated(orderId=123)
must create:
exactly one order
even if the event is delivered ten times.
________________________________________
3. The first protection: database invariant
Don't rely only on Java code.
Put the business invariant into the database:
CREATE UNIQUE INDEX ux_orders_order_id
ON orders(order_id);
Now:
M
 ↓
create order 123
 ↓
success
Redelivery:
M
 ↓
create order 123
 ↓
UNIQUE constraint violation
The database prevents the second order.
This is much stronger than:
if (!orderRepository.exists(orderId)) {
    orderRepository.create(orderId);
}
because exists() and create() are two separate operations.
________________________________________
4. Why check-then-act is dangerous
Consider:
if (!orderRepository.exists(orderId)) {
    orderRepository.create(order);
}
Two consumer instances:
Consumer A                  Consumer B

exists(123) → false         exists(123) → false
       |                            |
       ↓                            ↓
   create(123)                 create(123)
Both observed:
"doesn't exist"
before either created it.
This is a race condition.
The database's unique constraint solves the race:
A → INSERT 123 → success
B → INSERT 123 → duplicate key
Only one wins.
So remember:
Application checks can express an invariant, but the database should enforce important invariants when possible.
________________________________________
5. But business uniqueness isn't always message uniqueness
This is where we need the Inbox / Processed Message pattern.
Suppose:
OrderCreated
eventId = EVT-123
orderId = 42
We may want to remember:
EVT-123 was already consumed
Create:
CREATE TABLE processed_messages (
    message_id VARCHAR(100) PRIMARY KEY,
    processed_at TIMESTAMP NOT NULL
);
Now we have two concepts:
orders
---------
order_id
...


processed_messages
------------------
message_id
...
They protect different things.
orders
Protects:
Don't create two orders.
processed_messages
Protects:
Don't process the same logical message twice.
Those aren't necessarily the same invariant.
________________________________________
6. The critical transaction
Here's the core pattern:
@Transactional
public void consume(OrderCreated event) {

    recordMessage(event.eventId());

    createOrder(event);
}
Conceptually:
BEGIN TRANSACTION

INSERT processed_messages(eventId)

INSERT order(...)

COMMIT
Now we have atomicity.
Either:
processed message
+
business effect
both commit.
Or neither commits.
________________________________________
7. Why atomicity matters
Imagine the transaction did not exist.
record message
   ↓
COMMIT
   ↓
💥 crash
   ↓
create order never happened
Next delivery:
event arrives
   ↓
"already processed"
   ↓
ignore
You've lost the business operation.
That's obviously wrong.
Reverse the order:
create order
   ↓
COMMIT
   ↓
record message
   ↓
💥 crash
Next delivery:
create order again
Potential duplicate.
So:
Deduplication state and business state must be committed atomically whenever they live in the same transactional database.
This is one of the most important patterns in reliable consumers.
________________________________________
8. A production-style flow
Conceptually:
                 Message
                    |
                    v
          ┌───────────────────┐
          │ Extract event ID  │
          └─────────┬─────────┘
                    |
                    v
          ┌───────────────────┐
          │ BEGIN transaction │
          └─────────┬─────────┘
                    |
                    v
          ┌───────────────────┐
          │ Insert event ID   │
          │ into inbox table  │
          └─────────┬─────────┘
                    |
              duplicate?
              /          \
            yes           no
             |             |
             v             v
          rollback      business
          /ignore       operation
                           |
                           v
                      update DB
                           |
                           v
                        COMMIT
                           |
                           v
                          ACK
The important ordering is:
DB transaction
      ↓
COMMIT
      ↓
ACK broker
not:
ACK
 ↓
DB transaction
________________________________________
9. Why ACK must come after COMMIT
Consider:
DB transaction
      ↓
ACK
      ↓
commit
Suppose:
DB transaction
      ↓
ACK succeeds
      ↓
💥 JVM crash
      ↓
DB transaction rolls back
Broker thinks:
message completed
But business state wasn't committed.
Message is lost.
Therefore:
business state committed
      ↓
ACK
is the safer direction.
________________________________________
10. The fundamental consumer contract
For an at-least-once consumer, the desired sequence is:
Receive
   ↓
Begin DB transaction
   ↓
Deduplicate
   ↓
Perform business changes
   ↓
Commit
   ↓
ACK
And the key guarantee becomes:
If ACK happens,
the durable business state has already been committed.
That's the invariant you want.
________________________________________
11. But what happens if ACK fails?
This is where at-least-once comes back.
DB COMMIT
   ↓
ACK
   ↓
network failure
Broker doesn't know the ACK succeeded.
So:
redelivery
Consumer receives:
EVT-123
again.
Inbox table:
EVT-123 already exists
Therefore:
ignore duplicate
ACK
And we get:
10 deliveries
      ↓
1 business effect
That's the goal.
________________________________________
12. Java implementation
A simplified Spring-style implementation:
@Transactional
public void consume(OrderCreated event) {

    boolean inserted = inboxRepository.insertIfAbsent(
        event.eventId()
    );

    if (!inserted) {
        return; // duplicate
    }

    orderRepository.create(
        event.orderId(),
        event.customerId()
    );
}
The database might implement:
INSERT INTO processed_messages(message_id, processed_at)
VALUES (?, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP)
ON CONFLICT (message_id) DO NOTHING;
or the equivalent for your database.
Then:
insert = 1
means:
first processing
and:
insert = 0
means:
duplicate
________________________________________
13. But there's an important Spring/JPA detail
If you're using Spring:
@Transactional
public void consume(...)
only helps if the transaction is actually active.
For example, this can surprise people:
public void consume(Event event) {
    this.process(event);
}

@Transactional
private void process(Event event) {
    ...
}
Spring proxy-based transaction management can be bypassed by internal/self invocation.
Likewise, transaction configuration, propagation, exception handling, and listener acknowledgment behavior all matter.
So in production, don't merely look at:
@Transactional
and assume atomicity.
Verify:
What transaction starts?
What resources participate?
When does it commit?
When does listener ACK happen?
What exception causes rollback?
This is system design, not annotation memorization.
________________________________________
14. Exception handling can accidentally destroy reliability
Consider:
public void consume(Event event) {
    try {
        process(event);
    } catch (Exception e) {
        log.error("Failed", e);
    }
}
Looks harmless.
But suppose:
process()
   ↓
DB failure
   ↓
exception
   ↓
catch
   ↓
return normally
Your messaging framework may interpret:
consumer returned successfully
as:
ACK
Now the message can be lost.
So:
Don't swallow processing failures unless you intentionally want to acknowledge the message.
Usually you want the failure to propagate into the listener/container's error-handling mechanism.
________________________________________
15. The consumer needs a failure policy
Not every exception should cause infinite retries.
Suppose:
OrderCreated
contains invalid data:
customerId = null
Retrying 100 times won't fix it.
That's a poison message.
You need to distinguish:
Transient failure
Example:
database temporarily unavailable
network timeout
downstream service unavailable
Potential action:
retry
Permanent failure
Example:
invalid schema
missing required field
business rule violation
corrupt message
Potential action:
dead-letter queue
________________________________________
16. Retry isn't the same as redelivery
This distinction from Lesson 2 becomes operationally important.
Redelivery
Broker sends:
M
again.
Retry
Consumer/system says:
"I'll attempt processing M again."
You can have:
redelivery
   ↓
retry
   ↓
failure
   ↓
redelivery
   ↓
retry
If both mechanisms independently retry, you can accidentally multiply attempts.
For example:
Broker retry: 5 attempts
Consumer retry: 5 attempts
Potentially:
5 × 5 = 25
attempts, depending on implementation.
So define retry ownership clearly.
________________________________________
17. Backoff
Suppose downstream service is unavailable.
Bad:
retry immediately
retry immediately
retry immediately
retry immediately
Better:
1s
2s
4s
8s
16s
This is exponential backoff.
But if 10,000 consumers all do exactly:
1s
2s
4s
8s
they synchronize.
That's the thundering herd problem.
So add jitter:
delay = exponentialBackoff + randomJitter
Conceptually:
Consumer A → 4.3 sec
Consumer B → 5.1 sec
Consumer C → 3.7 sec
Consumer D → 4.8 sec
instead of all retrying at exactly 4 seconds.
________________________________________
18. Dead-letter queues
Eventually:
retry
retry
retry
retry
should stop.
A dead-letter queue gives you:
Main Queue
     |
     v
Consumer
     |
     X
 failure
     |
     v
Retry policy
     |
     X
max attempts
     |
     v
Dead Letter Queue
DLQ isn't a garbage dump.
It should preserve enough information to investigate:
event ID
event type
original payload
failure reason
attempt count
timestamp
producer metadata
The operational question becomes:
What do we do with a message that cannot currently be processed?
________________________________________
19. Don't blindly retry everything
Suppose:
paymentService.charge();
throws:
InvalidCardException
Retrying 20 times is pointless.
But:
SocketTimeoutException
may be transient.
So classify failures.
Conceptually:
Exception
   |
   +---- transient → retry
   |
   +---- permanent → DLQ
   |
   +---- unknown → cautious retry + alert
In real systems, the classification itself is part of the architecture.
________________________________________
20. Now the difficult case: external APIs
Suppose your transaction does:
@Transactional
public void consume(PaymentRequested event) {

    paymentRepository.markProcessing(event.id());

    paymentClient.charge(event.amount());

    paymentRepository.markPaid(event.id());
}
This looks reasonable.
But:
DB transaction
     |
     +---- markProcessing
     |
     +---- HTTP POST /charge
     |
     +---- markPaid
The HTTP request isn't part of your database transaction.
Suppose:
HTTP POST
   ↓
payment provider charges card
   ↓
response lost
   ↓
consumer throws timeout
Your DB transaction rolls back.
Broker redelivers.
Consumer sends:
POST /charge
again.
Potential double charge.
The database transaction did not solve the distributed side effect.
________________________________________
21. Idempotency keys solve this class of problem
Give the external operation a stable identity:
paymentOperationId = PAY-12345
Then:
POST /payments
Idempotency-Key: PAY-12345
If the same request arrives again:
PAY-12345
PAY-12345
PAY-12345
the payment service recognizes:
same logical operation
and returns the original result rather than performing another charge.
This is the external equivalent of our inbox table.
________________________________________
22. Think of idempotency as a protocol
This is a deeper way to understand it.
Producer:
"Perform operation X with identity K."
Consumer/service:
"If K has already succeeded,
return the previous result."
So:
Request
  |
  | K
  v
┌─────────────────┐
│ Idempotency     │
│ store           │
└─────────────────┘
The key isn't just:
UUID
The key represents:
one logical business operation.
That's why choosing the idempotency key is a domain decision.
________________________________________
23. Inbox and Outbox are mirror concepts
This is worth understanding deeply.
Inbox
Protects against:
incoming duplicate messages
Broker
   ↓
Consumer
   ↓
Inbox
   ↓
Business DB
Outbox
Protects against:
outgoing message loss
Business DB
   ↓
Outbox
   ↓
Publisher
   ↓
Broker
So:
INBOX
incoming reliability

OUTBOX
outgoing reliability
Together they form a powerful distributed messaging architecture.
________________________________________
24. Why outbox is needed
Suppose:
@Transactional
public void createOrder(Order order) {

    orderRepository.save(order);

    kafkaProducer.send(
        new OrderCreated(order.id())
    );
}
Two independent systems:
Database
+
Kafka
Suppose:
DB COMMIT
   ↓
💥 JVM crashes
   ↓
Kafka send never happens
Order exists.
Event doesn't.
Or:
Kafka send succeeds
   ↓
DB transaction rolls back
Now consumers received an event for an order that doesn't exist.
You have the classic dual-write problem.
________________________________________
25. Transactional outbox
Instead:
BEGIN DB TRANSACTION

INSERT order

INSERT outbox_event

COMMIT
Both are in the same database.
Then:
Outbox Publisher
      ↓
reads unpublished events
      ↓
publishes to broker
      ↓
marks published
Architecture:
                 DB
        ┌───────────────────┐
        │ Order              │
        │ Outbox             │
        └─────────┬─────────┘
                  |
                  v
            Outbox Publisher
                  |
                  v
                Broker
Now the database becomes the durable source of truth for the outgoing event.
________________________________________
26. But the outbox publisher itself can duplicate
Suppose:
read outbox event
   ↓
publish to Kafka
   ↓
Kafka success
   ↓
💥 publisher crashes
   ↓
mark published never happens
On restart:
publish same event again
So even the outbox publisher typically becomes:
at-least-once
And downstream consumers need to tolerate duplicates.
Notice the recurring pattern:
at-least-once
      ↓
duplicates
      ↓
idempotency
It keeps appearing at every boundary.
________________________________________
27. This leads to an important architectural principle
Don't attempt to build:
"no duplicates anywhere"
Instead:
Every boundary
     ↓
assume retry
     ↓
assume duplicate
     ↓
make operation idempotent
This is much more realistic.
________________________________________
28. Ordering is another consumer responsibility
Suppose events:
E1 version=10
E2 version=11
E3 version=12
arrive:
E1
E3
E2
If your consumer blindly applies them:
10
12
11
final state can be wrong.
A common strategy is optimistic version checking:
UPDATE orders
SET status = ?, version = ?
WHERE order_id = ?
  AND version < ?;
Conceptually:
current version = 12
incoming version = 11
then:
ignore
This protects against stale messages.
________________________________________
29. Duplicate + ordering together
The worst sequence isn't merely:
M
M
It's:
M1
M2
M1
M3
M2
where:
M1 version 1
M2 version 2
M3 version 3
Your consumer therefore may need both:
deduplication
+
version ordering
These solve different problems.
Deduplication
Have I already processed this exact event?
Versioning
Is this event older than the state I already have?
________________________________________
30. Consumer concurrency
Suppose one queue has:
10,000 messages
You want throughput.
So you create:
Consumer thread 1
Consumer thread 2
Consumer thread 3
...
Consumer thread N
Now the duplicate problem becomes concurrent.
Two workers can receive the same logical event:
Worker A → M
Worker B → M
Your consumer must therefore be correct under:
parallel execution
not just:
sequential retries
This is why database constraints and atomic operations are so important.
________________________________________
31. Don't hold database transactions across slow external calls
This is a common design mistake:
@Transactional
public void consume(Event event) {

    saveSomething();

    slowExternalApiCall(); // 10 seconds

    saveSomethingElse();
}
Now your database transaction remains open for 10 seconds.
Potential consequences:
locks held
connections occupied
connection pool exhaustion
higher contention
lower throughput
Worse, the external API still isn't transactionally part of your DB transaction.
So you've paid the transaction cost without gaining distributed atomicity.
Prefer carefully designed state machines/workflows for long-running external operations.
________________________________________
32. State machines are often better than one giant transaction
Instead of:
RECEIVED
   ↓
charge
   ↓
reserve
   ↓
ship
   ↓
DONE
inside one giant consumer invocation, model durable states:
PAYMENT_PENDING
      ↓
PAYMENT_CONFIRMED
      ↓
INVENTORY_RESERVED
      ↓
READY_TO_SHIP
Each transition can be:
durable
retryable
idempotent
observable
Now if the service crashes:
current state = PAYMENT_CONFIRMED
you know where to resume.
This is especially useful for sagas and long-running workflows.
________________________________________
33. Observability is part of safe consumer design
A production consumer should let you answer:
"Why was this message processed three times?"
You want identifiers such as:
eventId
correlationId
causationId
consumer name
attempt number
partition
offset / delivery metadata
And metrics:
messages processed
duplicate messages
retry count
DLQ count
processing latency
failure rate
redelivery count
Without observability, at-least-once systems can look like random chaos.
________________________________________
34. Logging must distinguish duplicate from failure
Bad:
ERROR processing message
for every duplicate.
A duplicate may be expected behavior.
Better conceptual categories:
INFO/WARN:
duplicate event ignored

ERROR:
processing failed

CRITICAL:
invariant violation
This matters operationally because otherwise your monitoring becomes noisy.
________________________________________
35. A complete safe-consumer mental model
Let's put everything together.
                    MESSAGE
                       |
                       v
               Validate envelope
                       |
                       v
                 Extract eventId
                       |
                       v
              BEGIN DB TRANSACTION
                       |
                       v
             Insert inbox record
                 /           \
              duplicate       new
                |               |
                v               v
             ignore       business logic
                                |
                       ┌────────┴─────────┐
                       |                  |
                       v                  v
